    },
    {
      "id": "claim_8f8b060b5f8b4a06875647740ab0abd2",
      "statement": "`normalizePointSelections` runs on both commute contexts on every successful read, in this order: dedupe `busStops` and `subwayStations` by id via a `Map` (last occurrence wins); drop `selectedBusStopIds` entries that do not resolve to a known deduped stop; cap the surviving non-empty list at `MAX_SELECTED_BUS_STOPS`; if the list ended up empty — stored empty or entirely dangling — fall back to `[busStops[0].id]` when any stop is saved, else an empty list; and let `selectedSubwayStationId` survive only if it matches a saved station, otherwise fall back to `subwayStations[0]?.id ?? null`. Net effect: a user with saved points always has at least one watched point per mode, so the arrivals panel is never blank for a non-empty commute.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://apps/web/src/hooks/transitSelectionStorage.ts#L37-L76",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:67e2991127c4abe5e5a036a10d26c9361931414de19680137e1e9c21125c8d4a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_65022da3e6b843eca99976e37fe66416",
      "statement": "`saveTransitSelections` is deliberately dumber than its read counterpart: it `JSON.stringify`s the document under the same storage key with no validation and no normalization, so the invariants are enforced on read rather than on write — writers may be sloppy, every successful read re-establishes the contract, and the stored document is allowed to be ragged (duplicate rows, stale ids, over-cap lists) while the in-memory document never is.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://apps/web/src/hooks/transitSelectionStorage.ts#L98-L104",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6248ee6a88726d85989eacba4cbb5e3bd5893ad0ddbd08563aa50ee00be7ed26:"
        }
      ]
    },
